.\" mount.gitmount(8) manual page — semantics per rfc/0000-gitmount.md (Q1-Q36; 2026-09-25 Tier-2 cache amendment)
.TH MOUNT.GITMOUNT 8 "2026-09-26" "gitmount 0.0.3" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount.gitmount \- mount a git repository as a read\-only FUSE filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount.gitmount
.RB [ options ]
.I repository
.I mountpoint
.br
.B mount
.BI \-t \ gitmount
.RB [ options ]
.I repository
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount.gitmount
mounts a local git repository (bare or non\-bare, sha1 or sha256) as a
.I read\-only
FUSE filesystem.
Every branch, tag, remote\-tracking ref, and commit is presented as a
directory containing the full snapshot of its tree, so any revision can be
browsed with ordinary tools
.RB ( ls ,
.BR cat ,
.BR grep ,
diff tools) without
.BR git\ checkout ,
.BR git\ worktree ,
or
.BR git\ archive .
.PP
The repository is never modified: all write operations fail with
.BR EROFS ,
and the mount is forcibly
.BR ro ", " nosuid ", " nodev
with
.BR default_permissions .
Object access is provided by libgit2; no
.B git
subprocesses are spawned and no network access is performed.
.SH INVOCATION
.B mount.gitmount
follows the
.BR mount (8)
helper convention
.RI ( mount.<type> )
and is installed in the system sbin directory, so it is invoked in one of
three equivalent ways:
.TP
.BI mount \-t \ gitmount \ repository \ mountpoint
.BR mount (8)
execs the helper per the helper contract
.RI ( "[-sfnv] [-N namespace] [-o options] [-t type.subtype] src dir" ):
the short options
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-v
(plus
.BI \-N \ ns
when
.B \-\-namespace
is used) and the
.B \-o
options string, with the options placed after the two positional
arguments (a measured argv is
.RI "src dir \-f \-o rw" ),
so the helper's parser tolerates option permutation (GNU getopt
style); behavior verified against util\-linux 2.42.3 libmount,
including real exec'd\-helper argv capture.
.B \-\-fake
is forwarded to the helper as
.BR \-f ;
.B \-r
and
.B \-w
are never forwarded as flags \(em libmount folds them into the
.B \-o
string as
.B ro
or
.B rw
\(em and a call without explicit read\-only intent (no
.B \-r
and no
.BR \-o\ ro )
always reaches the helper with
.B rw
already in its options string, so a plain
.B "mount \-t gitmount repo dir"
invokes
.BR "mount.gitmount repo dir \-o rw" ,
which the helper accepts with a warning (see
.B \-o
under OPTIONS).
A same\-string rw/ro conflict (e.g.\&
.BR \-o\ rw,ro )
is folded by libmount to a single key (measured on 2.42.3: the latter
wins, so
.B rw,ro
reaches the helper as a lone
.BR ro )
\(em the helper never receives both rw and ro from
.BR mount (8)
and needs no combination parsing of its own; an fstab
.B ro
overridden by a command\-line
.B \-o\ rw
is likewise resolved to a single forwarded key at the libmount layer,
and whichever key arrives is a no\-op (rw is warned) \(em the mount is
ro regardless, per the hardcoded baseline.
The contract's final optional clause,
.BI \-t \ type.subtype ,
carries the full dotted filesystem type when one is used (verified
empirically on util\-linux 2.42.3: a dotted type reaches the helper's
argv as
.RI "src dir \-o rw \-t type.subtype" ;
a dotless type never produces a \-t argument at all).
For the dotless
.I gitmount
type it never arrives via
.BR mount (8) ,
but it is handled for contract completeness: an arriving
.BI \-t \ gitmount
(only reachable on a direct invocation) is accepted and noted with one
verbose line, while any other value \-\- including a dotted
.I gitmount.<x>
that
.BR mount (8)
does forward when invoked as
.B mount \-t\ gitmount.<x>
\-\- is a usage error, since the helper's filesystem type is
.IR gitmount .
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
entry
.RS
.nf
/path/to/repo  /mnt/gitmount  gitmount  ro,blob\-cache\-size=128  0  0
.fi
.RE
.IP
Only a fixed subset of generic VFS options is filtered by
.BR mount (8)
before the helper is exec'd (auto, noauto, comment=, x\-*, loop,
offset=, sizelimit=, defaults, and the propagation options); every
other VFS option reaches the helper verbatim in the
.B \-o
string \(em the atime family (atime, noatime, relatime,
strictatime, lazytime, diratime, nodiratime), the sync family (sync,
async, dirsync), the exec family (exec, noexec), the user family (user,
users, owner, group, nouser \(em user and users implicitly add noexec, nosuid,
nodev, while owner and group only add nosuid, nodev), symfollow and
nosymfollow, iversion, silent,
loud, mand, nomand, nofs, _netdev, nofail, remount, uid=/gid=/umask=,
and the SELinux label keys context=/fscontext=/defcontext=/rootcontext=
(values contain colons), plus acl, quiet, showexec, and bsdgroups
(these four are not in the filesystem\-independent options table \(em
quiet and showexec are FAT options listed under FILESYSTEM\-SPECIFIC
mount options, acl appears only in fs\-specific sections, and
bsdgroups appears nowhere in the mount(8) manual; they arrive because
libmount forwards unknown out\-of\-table keys as fs\-specific data)
\(em and every arriving option is
classified as described under
.BR OPTIONS .
With util\-linux >= 2.35 a command\-line
.B \-o
augments the fstab options (the merged string may list the same key
twice); gitmount\-specific keys then follow last\-one\-wins ordering
(see OPTIONS).
.TP
.B mount.gitmount
.I repository mountpoint
Direct invocation; identical semantics.
.PP
Unmount with
.BR umount (8)
or
.BR fusermount3 (1).
.SH MOUNT LAYOUT
.TP
.I branch/
Local branches.
Branch names containing
.B /
(e.g.\&
.IR feature/foo )
are rendered as nested directories.
Resolution takes the longest ref-name match, so a path has at most one
ref interpretation even in a pathological repository whose hand-edited
packed-refs file breaks the ref database's D/F rule (refs/tags/foo
coexisting with refs/tags/foo/bar):
.I /tag/foo/bar
resolves to the
.I refs/tags/foo/bar
ref rather than to entry
.I bar
inside the
.I refs/tags/foo
snapshot, a same-name tree entry and child ref
collapse to one item rendered as the ref, and the ambiguity is logged
as a warning (the same fallback covers tags and remote-tracking refs;
normal ref-database writes never produce this state).
A node that is itself a complete ref with child refs beneath it
(a merged node) lists the union of its snapshot tree entries and the
child ref names, folding a same-name collision in favor of the child
ref; if that ref does not peel to a commit, lookup semantics prevail
\(em the node itself is
.B ENOENT
even though the parent listing still shows the folded directory
entry (the shape mismatch is logged as a warning; a listing entry is
enumeration only and never a promise of accessibility).
A branch whose ref does not peel to a commit (e.g.\& manually
pointed at a blob or tree with
.BR "git update\-ref" )
is not presented
.RB ( ENOENT ,
with a warning logged).
.TP
.I tag/
All tags, annotated and lightweight.
A tag that points directly to a tree or blob object is not resolvable:
access yields
.B ENOENT
(with a warning logged).
Such a tag may still appear in the
.I tag/
listing \(em listings enumerate the ref database and are enumeration
hints, not access promises (the same shape\-mismatch rule as merged
nodes).
.TP
.I commit/
Arbitrary commits by full object id:
.I commit/<oid>
where
.I <oid>
is 40 lowercase hex chars (sha1) or 64 (sha256) \(em uppercase hex is
rejected with
.B ENOENT
\(em naming a commit object itself
\(em no peeling, no abbreviation.
Listing
.I commit/
always returns an empty directory (commits are not enumerable).
To resolve a short prefix, grep the
.I commits
listing file instead.
.TP
.I remote/
Remote\-tracking refs
.IR remote/<remote>/<branch> ,
read directly from
.IR refs/remotes/ .
The symbolic ref
.I refs/remotes/<remote>/HEAD
is hidden (access yields
.BR ENOENT );
a non\-symbolic ref written directly at that path (with
.B "git update\-ref"
) is not hidden and is treated as an ordinary tracking ref;
a ref that does not peel to a commit is likewise not presented
.RB ( ENOENT ).
Namespace existence follows the ref database: a namespace whose only
current content is that hidden
.B HEAD
still renders as an
.B empty
directory (not suppressed), and fills in as soon as any tracking ref
appears.
A bare namespace ref \(em a complete ref written directly at
.IR refs/remotes/<x>
(by
.BR "git update\-ref" )
with no refs beneath it \(em is likewise treated as a namespace, not
as a presentable ref:
.I remote/<x>
renders as an empty synthetic namespace directory, and the ref's own
commit snapshot is not reachable through
.I remote/
(an implicit consequence of the two\-level
.I <remote>/<branch>
mapping, unlike the deliberate hiding of symbolic
.BR HEAD ;
the
.I commits
listing still includes it, since it enumerates every ref under
.IR refs/ ).
That unreachability holds only while nothing sits beneath the ref:
if child refs do coexist under it (only constructible through a
hand\-edited packed\-refs file), the node instead follows the
merged\-node rule described under
.IR branch/ ,
and the ref body is reachable again through lookup.
.TP
.I HEAD/
Snapshot of the current
.BR HEAD .
An unborn
.B HEAD
yields
.BR ENOENT ;
so does a HEAD pointing at a non\-commit object (same rule as
tags and branches).
.TP
.I commits
Read\-only text file listing the full oid of every commit reachable from
all refs
.B and
.BR HEAD
(every ref under
.I refs/
, including notes, stash, and replace namespaces \(em matching
.B "git \-\-no\-replace\-objects rev\-list \-\-all"
\(em plus detached\-HEAD\-only commits; refs that do not peel to a
commit, such as a lightweight tag on a blob or tree, are skipped with
a verbose log entry and are not an error), one per line, in deterministic
topological order
(GIT_SORT_TOPOLOGICAL; refs are pushed in sorted ref\-name order, HEAD
last, so the relative order of parallel commits is reproducible byte
for byte).
It is generated lazily on first
.BR open ;
before that
.B stat
reports size 0.
On the first
.B open
the generation runs a full revwalk: on huge repositories (millions of
commits) this can take seconds, and the open blocks for that long.
Each
.B open
pins one snapshot version for the lifetime of the file handle, so refs
changing mid\-read never corrupt a reader.
.TP
.I .gitmount.json
Mount metadata: format version, absolute repository path,
.B head
(ref name; full oid when detached; null when unborn),
mount timestamp, and the configured cache sizes in bytes
.RB ( cache.blob_bytes ,
.BR cache.tree_bytes ).
If the repository path contains bytes that are not valid UTF\-8, the
.I repository
field percent\-escapes them as
.BI % XX
(ASCII and valid multi\-byte sequences are kept verbatim) so the file
remains valid JSON; the escaping is not reversible (a literal
.BI % XX
already present in the path is indistinguishable from an escaped
byte).
It is an immutable snapshot taken at mount time and does not track later
HEAD movement \(em use
.I HEAD/
or
.B git\ rev\-parse
for live HEAD.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o \ option[,option...]
Comma\-separated options, the mount(8)\-style form of the long options
below.
The gitmount\-specific keys
.BI blob\-cache\-size= MiB
and
.BI tree\-cache\-size= MiB
(dash and underscore spellings are equivalent) have exactly the same
semantics and validation as the corresponding long options.
If the same key is given more than once (within one \-o string, across
fstab and command line \(em util\-linux >= 2.35 merges them \(em or
mixed with the long options in command\-line order), the last
occurrence wins, matching mount(8) conventions.
Keys synonymous with the hardcoded baseline
.RB ( ro ,
.BR nosuid ,
.BR nodev ,
.BR default_permissions ,
.BR use_ino )
are accepted as redundant no\-ops.
.B rw
is likewise accepted as a no\-op, with a stderr warning: libmount
unconditionally places
.B rw
in the helper's options string for a call without read\-only intent,
so rejecting it would break the plain
.B "mount \-t gitmount repo dir"
invocation (ro stays hardcoded and cannot be diluted; ntfs\-3g uses
the same convention).
Generic VFS options that libmount forwards verbatim in the \-o
string (only the fixed subset named above is filtered before the
helper is exec'd) are likewise accepted and ignored, each noted by
one verbose line: the atime family (atime, noatime, relatime,
strictatime, lazytime, diratime, nodiratime), the sync family (sync,
async, dirsync), the exec family (exec, noexec), the user family
(user, users, owner, group, nouser), symfollow and nosymfollow,
iversion, silent, loud, mand, nomand,
nofs, _netdev, nofail, and the out\-of\-table forwarded keys acl,
quiet, showexec, and bsdgroups \(em all harmless under
the hardcoded
.B ro
and the atime≡mtime semantics, so common habits such as
.B "mount \-t gitmount \-o noatime,nodiratime repo dir"
and fstab entries with
.B user
(for non\-root mounting) or
.B "nofail / _netdev"
(common at boot) keep working (rw/ntfs\-3g precedent).
List matching is by key name, cut at the first
.BR = :
valued spellings such as
.BI user= alice
or
.BI nofail= 1
are matched by key name alone (the value is not inspected); the
util\-linux >= 2.41 qualified forms
.BI ro= vfs
and
.BI rw= fs
(and recursive spellings) therefore match ro/rw and land on the
existing no\-op paths (ro is redundant, rw is warned) with no
special parsing of their own.
The classification is dual\-track, with the enumeration first: any key
named in the enumerated list above is classified by the list \(em
including keys absent from mount(8)'s filesystem\-independent options
table (nofs, symfollow \(em added by util\-linux >= 2.41 and still
absent from the 2.42.3 table \(em and acl, quiet, showexec, and
bsdgroups, which arrive only because libmount forwards unknown
out\-of\-table keys as fs\-specific data). Then the table track covers
only options listed in that table (see
the
.BR mount (8)
manual, "Filesystem\-independent mount options"): a listed option that
is a no\-op for gitmount \(em including in\-table negation keys not
individually enumerated, such as noiversion, norelatime, nostrictatime,
and nolazytime \(em is accepted and ignored;
table options that would change gitmount semantics or security intent
are usage errors; keys in neither track are forwarded to libfuse
(so a direct invocation with keys mount(8) itself filters \(em defaults,
auto, noauto, comment=, x\-*, loop, offset=, sizelimit=, and the
propagation options \(em is a usage error: these are mount(8)\-side
directives with no consumer on the direct path, an intentional
asymmetry with the mount(8) path, where they are filtered before the
helper is exec'd and always succeed).
When a future util\-linux release forwards a new key that the table
does not list (as 2.41's symfollow was), it is triaged by hand \(em
"is it a no\-op for gitmount?" \(em into the enumerated list, tracked in
the maintenance checklist
.IR docs/maintenance\-checklist.md
(on each util\-linux release: diff the man
table against the set of forwarded keys, triage, update the list and
the test cases), instead of falling through to libfuse rejection.
.BR remount ,
.BI uid= / gid= / umask= ,
and the SELinux label keys
.BI context= / fscontext= / defcontext= / rootcontext=
are usage errors with dedicated error messages (v0.1 has no remount,
ownership\-mapping, or security\-label semantics; silently ignoring
them would hide the user's intent \(em mounting with
.BI context=
is routine under SELinux and must fail loudly rather than silently
drop the label).
The remaining reverse keys
.RB ( suid ,
.B dev )
are usage errors, so the baseline cannot be diluted.
.BI fsname= name
may override the baseline fsname (cosmetic only);
.BI subtype= x
is baseline\-protected and overriding it is a usage error, since the
.I /proc/mounts
type field and
.B "mount \-t gitmount"
matching depend on it.
All other keys are forwarded verbatim to the libfuse option parser
(e.g.\&
.BR kernel_cache ;
.B allow_other
requires
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf );
unknown keys are rejected by libfuse (exit status 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-blob\-cache\-size \ MiB
Blob LRU cache limit in MiB (default 64).
A single blob at or above the limit (size >= limit; the boundary is
deliberately inclusive, so that a blob exactly equal to the limit
cannot evict the whole pool just to be inserted) bypasses the cache:
it is looked up
and fully decompressed once at
.BR open ,
the decompressed block is pinned on the file handle for the lifetime
of the open, and
.B release
frees it, so a sequential read decompresses exactly once (nothing is
evicted to make room).
Only the object lookup is performed under gitmount's internal lock; the
full decompression runs outside it, so other requests on the mount are
not stalled while it runs.
The
.B open
itself still waits for the whole decompression (seconds for multi-GB
blobs), the same way a first
.I commits
.B open
waits for its revwalk.
Cache\-miss loads of cacheable blobs use the same split (lookup plus
double\-check under the lock, decompression outside it, recheck before
inserting), so the lock is never held across a whole\-blob decompression
however large the cache is; concurrent first loads of the same blob may
transiently decompress twice (the loser's block is discarded).
The oversize test itself \-\- and every reported blob
.BR st_size \-\-
comes from a header\-only ODB read
.RB ( git_odb_read_header ),
so
.B stat
never triggers a blob decompression, just as it never triggers the
.I commits
revwalk.
The memory footprint of oversized blobs is the sum of their sizes
across concurrently open handles.
The cache limit bounds accounted payload bytes only; the daemon's
resident set is larger in practice (see
.BR \-\-tree\-cache\-size 
for the measured breakdown).
The value must be a positive integer; 0, negative, or unparseable
values are usage errors (exit status 1); there is no artificial upper
cap (bounded only by available memory).
.TP
.BI \-\-tree\-cache\-size \ MiB
Byte\-exact budget of gitmount's own metadata cache in MiB (default
256): trees are held as raw serialized bytes plus a 4\-byte\-per\-entry
offset index (entry names are zero\-copy views into the buffer), and
commits as compact facts tuples (root tree oid + committer time, with
tag chains resolved on demand).
Fixed per\-entry bookkeeping is charged against the budget too, so the
configured value is the real cap of this component (libgit2's parsed
object cache is fully disabled).
Together with mmap'ed packfile pages (file\-backed, shared and
reclaimable under pressure) and the st_ino registry, the daemon's RSS
therefore exceeds the sum of the two cache knobs — but each part is
bounded and accounted.
Validation is identical to
.BR \-\-blob\-cache\-size .
Tree/commit metadata is served from this separate internal cache and is
not governed by
.BR \-\-blob\-cache\-size ;
the two caches account independently and never evict each other.
.TP
.BR \-\-foreground
Run in the foreground (default: daemonize after mounting).
Long option only: the short
.B \-f
is reserved for the mount(8) helper contract (fake mode, below).
.TP
.B \-f
Fake mode \(em the form in which
.BR mount (8)'s
.B \-\-fake
is forwarded to helpers (libmount's exec_helper passes
.BR \-f ).
All arguments and options are fully validated, including opening
the repository (an unreadable or non\-git repository exits with
status 2); only the mount and the daemonization are skipped, so a
fake run does not green\-light an fstab entry that would fail only
at boot time.
On success the helper exits with status 0 without mounting anything.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log path resolution, cache hits, and blob decompression events to
stderr (one line per full decompression, covering both oversized
open\-pin decompressions and cache\-miss LRU loads; a plain
.B "ls \-l"
produces no decompression lines since blob sizes are read from ODB
headers only).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-s ", " "\-N ns"
Accepted and ignored (no\-mtab / sloppy / namespace flags forwarded by
.BR mount (8):
.B \-\-namespace
reaches the helper as
.BI \-N \ ns
per the helper contract, although
.BR mount (8)
currently refuses to switch namespaces before exec'ing a helper).
Note that
.B \-s
does not relax unknown\-key rejection: mount(8) documents sloppy as
ignoring mount options not supported by the filesystem, but silently
swallowing mistyped gitmount\-specific keys would hide configuration
errors, so unknown \-o keys are still classified as described above.
.TP
.BI \-t \ type
Forwarded by
.BR mount (8)
only for a dotted filesystem type (a dotless type such as
.I gitmount
never produces a \-t argument; a dotted
.I gitmount.<x>
is forwarded verbatim), so this is chiefly reachable on a direct
invocation.
.BI \-t \ gitmount
is accepted and noted with one verbose line (a redundant
self-reference); any other value is a usage error, since the helper's
filesystem type is
.IR gitmount .
.TP
.B \-r ", " \-w
Never received from
.BR mount (8) :
libmount does not forward them as flags, it appends
.B ro
or
.B rw
to the
.B \-o
string instead (\-r / \-o ro are redundant no\-ops; \-o rw is a
warned no\-op, see above).
Passing them explicitly on a direct invocation is a usage error
(the filesystem is read\-only either way).
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-\-help
Print version / usage and exit.
.SH SEMANTICS
.SS Read\-only data plane
Every mutating syscall returns
.BR EROFS ;
the mount is
.B ro\ +\ nosuid\ +\ nodev
and the kernel performs standard permission checks
.RB ( default_permissions ).
.SS Extended attributes
No xattr operation is registered, so
.BR getxattr ,
.BR listxattr ,
and friends report
.B ENOTSUP
(including the
.I security.*
queries that statx issues under SELinux).
.SS File metadata
Directories are 0755, plain files 0644, executables 0755, symlinks 0777.
The synthetic files
.I commits
and
.I .gitmount.json
are mode 0444 (nlink 1); the root directory and the five entry
directories
.BR branch/ ,
.BR tag/ ,
.BR remote/ ,
.BR commit/ ,
and
.I HEAD/
report the mount time as mtime/ctime/atime (nlink 2).
The synthetic grouping directories (nested\-ref prefixes such as
.IR branch/feature ,
.IR tag/v1.0 ,
and remote namespace directories such as
.IR remote/origin )
follow the same rule \(em 0755, nlink 2, mount time \(em not the
committer time of any tree.
All directories, the root included, report
.B st_size
4096, matching
.BR st_blksize .
.BR st_mtime ", " st_ctime ,
and
.B st_atime
of tree content are all the committer time of the underlying commit
(constant within a snapshot; atime never advances on read).
.I commits
carries its generation time
.RB ( mount
time until first generated, while
.B stat
still reports size 0; after a fingerprint rebuild, stat reports the
current cached version's time),
.I .gitmount.json
the mount time.
Blob
.B st_size
is obtained from a header\-only ODB read
.RB ( git_odb_read_header ),
never from a full decompression: stat'ing a directory that contains
multi\-GB blobs costs no more than a header lookup, just as getattr
never triggers the
.I commits
revwalk.
A symlink's
.B st_size
is the length after truncation at the first embedded NUL, so
.B stat
always agrees with the length
.B readlink
returns (a NUL-containing symlink never reports its raw blob size);
computing it requires reading the (PATH_MAX-bounded) blob content to
locate the NUL, an explicit exception to the header-only path above
(that PATH_MAX bound holds only for git's own writes).
One further pathological form is an explicit exception to this
stat/readlink agreement: a hand-crafted symlink blob whose
truncation-semantics length is PATH_MAX or longer, with or without a
NUL (a NUL first appearing past PATH_MAX still truncates to a
too-long target; only constructible by hand), reports
.B st_size
as its truncation-semantics length while
.B readlink
itself fails with
.BR ENAMETOOLONG ;
the kernel accepts symlink targets of at most PATH_MAX\-1 bytes.
.PP
.B st_blocks
is always
.BI ceil ( st_size /512)
(512\-byte blocks; the synthetic files follow the same formula on
their content size),
.B st_blksize
is 4096, and
.B st_rdev
is 0.
libfuse does not derive st_blocks from st_size, and leaving it at its
default 0 would make
.B du
report zero blocks for every file.
.PP
.B st_ino
is a 64\-bit hash of the full path.
Hash collisions are resolved by a mount\-local registry, so two
different paths never share an inode; remount stability holds except
for the astronomically rare colliding paths (~3e\-8 per million
entries).
The registry registers paths lazily (only paths actually stat'ed or
listed) and never frees entries during the mount, so its memory grows
with the number of distinct paths touched and inode numbers are never
reused.
Equal inodes never imply hard links or shared content.
.SS Submodules
Rendered as an empty directory plus a sibling text file
.IR <name>\.gitmount\-submodule
(mode 0644) containing two lines,
.B url=<url>
and
.BI commit= <oid> ,
where the url comes from the
.I .gitmodules
blob at that commit (empty, with a warning, if absent).
.SS Symbolic links
readlink returns the raw blob content, truncated at the first embedded
NUL (kernel restriction, same as
.B "git checkout"
behavior); an empty blob yields an empty target;
st_size
reports the truncated length, matching the length readlink returns
(see File metadata).
One pathological form is excepted from this agreement: a blob whose
truncated length is PATH_MAX or longer, with or without any NUL
(only constructible by hand) fails readlink with
.B ENAMETOOLONG
while
.B st_size
still reports the truncated length (see File metadata).
Targets may point anywhere, including outside the snapshot.
.SS Object replacement
.B gitmount
does not honor
.IR refs/replace :
objects are always presented raw, matching
.B "git \-\-no\-replace\-objects"
semantics, so an oid maps to exactly one immutable object.
.SS Names
Tree entry names are passed through as raw bytes (non\-UTF\-8 names are
not rewritten); entries literally named
.B .
or
.B ..
are skipped.
All directory listings (root,
.BR branch/ ,
.BR tag/ ,
.BR remote/ ,
and tree directories, including the
.I .gitmount\-submodule
synthetic entries) are emitted in raw\-byte lexicographic order of each
path component (memcmp, locale\-independent); the merged\-node union of
tree entries and child\-ref names (see under
.IR branch/ )
joins that same single ordering, mixing tree entries and child\-ref
names without grouping by origin; tree entries are
re\-sorted rather than following git's intrinsic tree order (where
directory names compare as if suffixed with '/').
.B statfs
reports zero free blocks and zero free inodes, so
.B df
shows the mount as 100% used;
.I f_blocks
reflects the local object database size (packs + loose objects,
alternates excluded).
.SS Live updates
.BR branch/ ", " tag/ ", and " remote/
listings reflect external repository changes (new commits, tags, deleted
branches) without remounting.
Kernel attribute and entry cache timeouts are pinned to 0 to avoid stale
entries.
Users who know the repository will not change during a mount may opt in
with
.BR \-o\ kernel_cache ,
which affects only data page caching (read/mmap); attribute and entry
timeouts remain 0, and a metadata staleness window requires additionally
passing
.B attr_timeout
or
.BR entry_timeout .
.SS Empty repositories
A repository with no refs and an unborn
.B HEAD
mounts successfully:
.IR branch/ ,
.IR tag/ ,
and
.I remote/
are empty directories,
.I HEAD/
yields
.BR ENOENT ,
.I commits
is an empty file (size 0), and
.I .gitmount.json
reports
.B head
as null.
.SS Concurrent repository maintenance
.B gitmount
neither locks the repository nor blocks external git commands.
If
.B "git gc"
or
.B "git prune"
rewrites packs or removes objects that a pending request still needs,
that request transiently fails with
.B ENOENT
(object gone) or
.B EIO
(pack invalidated mid\-read), and recovers once gc finishes;
.B gitmount
does not promise a stable object set for the lifetime of a mount.
If a first\-time
.I commits
generation hits this mid\-revwalk, that
.B open
fails with
.BR EIO ;
the partial buffer is not cached and the fingerprint is left untouched,
so the next
.B open
retries from scratch.
When transient errors are unacceptable, disable automatic gc for the
duration of the mount
.RB ( git\ config\ gc.auto\ 0 ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful mount (the process exits 0 after daemonizing; this is the
exit status
.BR mount (8)
propagates for a helper invocation), a graceful foreground
termination (SIGINT/SIGTERM), or a successful fake run of
.B \-f
(arguments and the repository validated, nothing mounted).
Unmounting is performed by
.BR umount (8)
or
.BR fusermount3 (1)
and does not involve this program.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error (bad arguments, forbidden
.B \-o
keys).
.TP
.B 2
Repository unreadable or not a git repository.
.TP
.B 3
Mount failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mount \-t gitmount ~/src/myproj /mnt/gitmount
mount \-t gitmount \-o noatime,nodiratime ~/src/myproj /mnt/gitmount   # VFS no\-op keys accepted
mount \-\-fake \-t gitmount ~/src/myproj /mnt/gitmount   # forwarded as \-f: validate only
ls /mnt/gitmount/branch/main/src
grep ^0beec7b /mnt/gitmount/commits      # resolve a short prefix
cat /mnt/gitmount/commit/<full\-oid>/README.md
diff \-r /mnt/gitmount/tag/v1.0/src /mnt/gitmount/branch/main/src
fusermount3 \-u /mnt/gitmount            # or: umount /mnt/gitmount
.fi
.PP
/etc/fstab:
.nf
/path/to/repo  /mnt/gitmount  gitmount  ro,blob\-cache\-size=128,tree\-cache\-size=512  0  0
/path/to/repo  /mnt/gitmount  gitmount  ro,user,noatime  0  0    # non\-root mountable
.fi
.SH SECURITY
The kernel enforces ownership and permission checks
.RB ( default_permissions )
and the mount is read\-only without suid or device nodes.
Symlink targets and file names are repository\-controlled data and are
passed through unmodified; do not browse mounts of untrusted
repositories with root or other privileged accounts.
The repository path is canonicalized with
.B realpath
before use.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR git (1),
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR mount.fuse3 (8)
.PP
Full design and semantics:
.I rfc/0000\-gitmount.md
and
.I docs/filesystem\-semantics.md
in the gitmount source repository.
